INT. DRAGON'S HOARD, MOUNTAIN CAVERN - TWILIGHT

A cavern as wide as a cathedral. Gold rises in slow dunes to the ceiling, spilling goblets, helms, a toppled throne. Far above, a fissure lets in a shaft of rose light. Warm air smells of sulphur and old coins.

SER ALARIC THORNWELL, forty, battered mail under a cloak of faded blue, kneels on a gold dune with a lantern. Beside him, young BRANDOLF "BRAN" ASHDOWN, eighteen, his squire, hauls a sack, panting. Both are filthy with soot. And on a shelf of rock above them, curled around a brazier of coals, lies an elderly dragon, VESPERINE, scales gone the colour of dusk.

The dragon opens one yellow eye.

VESPERINE
You are very late.

Alaric lowers the lantern. Bran drops the sack.

ALARIC
Forgive us, Vesperine. The pass was snowed in.

VESPERINE
The pass has been snowed in for three hundred years. I would not have thought it a surprise.

BRAN
Ser Alaric, she's talking.

ALARIC
Yes, Bran.

BRAN
Dragons talk?

VESPERINE
Dragons do everything. We merely prefer not to.

Bran stares at her. He stares at Alaric. Alaric folds his arms.

ALARIC
We came for the sword.

VESPERINE
Everyone comes for the sword.

ALARIC
My lord is dying. The kingdom needs it.

VESPERINE
Your lord is a fool.

ALARIC
Perhaps. But he is mine.

The dragon rolls her head toward Bran.

VESPERINE
And you, little one. What do you come for?

BRAN
(whispering)
Me? I came to carry the sack.

VESPERINE
Honest, at least.

She shifts. The gold slides. A tarnished hilt appears among the coins. Alaric starts toward it. Vesperine's tail curls across his path, not violently, just a gate closing.

ALARIC
You'll not let us take it?

VESPERINE
I did not say that.

ALARIC
Then what?

VESPERINE
You carry a sack, a lantern, and the weight of a dying lord. Put them down. Sit.

Alaric looks at Bran. Bran shrugs. They sit on the gold, knees drawn up like boys. Vesperine settles her chin on her claws.

VESPERINE (CONT'D)
Tell me about the king.

ALARIC
He was a good man once.

VESPERINE
And now?

ALARIC
Now he is a tired one.

VESPERINE
Tired men make poor kings. But they make excellent grandfathers.

BRAN
(softly)
She's smiling.

ALARIC
Dragons do not smile.

VESPERINE
You see? You know nothing about us.

She lifts her tail. The sword gleams.

VESPERINE (CONT'D)
Take it. And tell your king the mountain remembers him kindly.

Alaric rises. He crosses the dunes, lifts the hilt with both hands, and bows over it. Bran trots after him, dragging the sack.

At the cavern mouth Bran glances back. The dragon's eye is closing. The shaft of rose light fades to grey.

BRAN
She let us go.

ALARIC
She did.

BRAN
Why?

ALARIC
(after a moment)
I think she was lonely, Bran.
